DURATION OF LEG MOVEMENTS AND FERRITIN LEVELS
Mack C,
1 Sharon D,3,2,1 Simakajornboon N,2 Shamsnia M,1,2 Ferriss G4,1
(1) Advanced Sleep Center, Metairie, LA, LA, USA, (2) Tulane
University Hospital & Medical Center, New Orleans, LA, USA, (3)
Premier Sleep Medicine Center, Baton Rouge, LA, USA, (4) Memorial
Medical Center, New Orleans, LA, USA
Introduction : Routine scoring and observation of polysomnographic
recordings suggests that leg movements appear to be of longer duration in
patients who have a low serum ferritin level. In order to assess these dif-
ferences, a retrospective study was performed on patients having a pri-
mary diagnosis of PLMD.
Methods : Data analysis was performed on patients who had one night of
polysomnography in our sleep center. The group included pediatric
(N=10), ages 1-13 years (mean 5.3 +/- 4.3) and adult (N=10), ages 27-58
(mean 44.6 +/- 9.7) patients. Baseline ferritin levels were obtained on all
patients. Leg EMG was recorded from standard sites on the anterior tib-
ialis. All polysomnographic recordings were scored for sleep stages, res-
piratory events, arousals, and PLM’s. Arousals were differentiated by
type: spontaneous, respiratory, snore, and PLM. Periodic limb movements
were scored for frequency and duration. Events associated with respirato-
ry events and PLM’s during wake were rejected. Patient groups were
assigned based on serum ferritin level: group I had a serum ferritin of
≤50ng/ml; group II had a serum ferritin of > 50 ng/ml.
Results : The duration of limb movements in Group I was significantly
longer than that of Group II (P<.01) with the mean for Group I being 4.4
(± 0.71) seconds versus 1.9 (± 0.21) seconds for Group II. The mean
serum ferritin for Group I was 22.5 (±10.8 ) as opposed to 120.5 (±54.2)
for Group II (P<.01). The mean PLM Index for Group I was 20.45(± 7.56)
and 30.72(± 13.8) for Group II (P<.05).
Conclusion : The data suggests that there may be a relationship between
low levels of serum ferritin (<50 ng/ml) and prolonged leg movements
with duration greater than 3 seconds. Further research, with larger num-
bers, is indicated to more adequately assess the characteristics of the
PLM’s in these populations.
